# Nmap — CPTS Cheat Sheet

> Practical Nmap notes for penetration testing, network enumeration, and HTB/CPTS labs.

---

## 1. Basic Nmap

| Command                  | Explanation                              |
| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------- |
| `nmap <TARGET>`          | Basic scan of common TCP ports           |
| `sudo nmap <TARGET>`     | Run Nmap with root privileges            |
| `nmap -v <TARGET>`       | Verbose output                           |
| `nmap -vv <TARGET>`      | Very verbose output                      |
| `nmap -n <TARGET>`       | Disable DNS resolution                   |
| `nmap -Pn <TARGET>`      | Skip host discovery; assume target is up |
| `nmap --reason <TARGET>` | Show why Nmap determined the port state  |

---

## 2. Host Discovery

| Command                        | Explanation                       |
| ------------------------------ | --------------------------------- |
| `sudo nmap -sn <TARGET>`       | Host discovery only; no port scan |
| `sudo nmap -PE <TARGET>`       | ICMP Echo host discovery          |
| `sudo nmap -Pn <TARGET>`       | Skip host discovery               |
| `sudo nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24` | Discover live hosts in a subnet   |
| `sudo nmap -iL hosts.txt`      | Scan targets from a file          |

### Remember

```text
-sn = Discover hosts
-Pn = Assume host is up
```

---

## 3. TCP Port Scanning

| Command                              | Explanation               |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| `sudo nmap -sS <TARGET>`             | TCP SYN scan              |
| `sudo nmap -sT <TARGET>`             | TCP Connect scan          |
| `sudo nmap -p 80 <TARGET>`           | Scan port 80              |
| `sudo nmap -p 22,80,443 <TARGET>`    | Scan selected ports       |
| `sudo nmap -p 1-1000 <TARGET>`       | Scan ports 1–1000         |
| `sudo nmap -p- <TARGET>`             | Scan all 65,535 TCP ports |
| `sudo nmap --top-ports 100 <TARGET>` | Scan top 100 common ports |

### SYN Scan Logic

```text
SYN
 │
 ├── SYN/ACK → OPEN
 │
 ├── RST     → CLOSED
 │
 └── No response → FILTERED
```

---

## 4. UDP Scanning

| Command                               | Explanation             |
| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| `sudo nmap -sU <TARGET>`              | UDP scan                |
| `sudo nmap -sU -p53 <TARGET>`         | Scan UDP port 53        |
| `sudo nmap -sU -p53,161,500 <TARGET>` | Scan selected UDP ports |
| `sudo nmap -sU -p- <TARGET>`          | Scan all UDP ports      |

### Common UDP Ports

```text
53    DNS
67/68 DHCP
69    TFTP
123   NTP
161   SNMP
500   ISAKMP
```

> UDP scanning is generally much slower than TCP scanning.

