## 13. Firewall / IDS / IPS Evasion

### ACK Scan

```bash
sudo nmap -sA -p443 <TARGET>
```

Primarily determines:

```text
FILTERED
    vs
UNFILTERED
```

It does not directly determine whether a port is open.

---

### Source Port Manipulation

```bash
sudo nmap -sS -p443 --source-port 53 <TARGET>
```

Use source port `53`.

Useful against poorly configured firewall rules that trust traffic originating from specific ports.

---

### Packet Fragmentation

```bash
sudo nmap -sS -f <TARGET>
```

Fragment IP packets.

```bash
sudo nmap -sS --mtu 8 <TARGET>
```

Specify the fragment size.

---

### Decoys

```bash
sudo nmap -sS -D RND:5 <TARGET>
```

Generate random decoy sources.

Conceptually:

```text
Your scan
   +
Decoy
   +
Decoy
   +
Decoy
```

---

### Source IP

```bash
sudo nmap -S <SOURCE_IP> <TARGET>
```

Specify a source IP.

---

### Network Interface

```bash
sudo nmap -e tun0 <TARGET>
```

Specify the network interface.

Useful when scanning through a VPN such as an HTB VPN interface.

---

## 14. Firewall States

| State              | Meaning                                            |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `open`             | A service is listening                             |
| `closed`           | Host is reachable but no service is listening      |
| `filtered`         | Filtering prevents Nmap from determining the state |
| `open\|filtered`   | Nmap cannot distinguish                            |
| `closed\|filtered` | Nmap cannot distinguish                            |

### Remember

```text
OPEN
  ↓
Service responds

CLOSED
  ↓
Host responds but no service

FILTERED
  ↓
Firewall/filter interferes
```

